@@@ problem 530 minimum-absolute-difference-in-bst
title: Minimum Absolute Difference in BST
difficulty: Easy
topics: Tree, Depth-First Search, Binary Search Tree, Binary Tree
params: root
time: O(n)
space: O(h)
bench: n=64..1048576 time=n space=1 unit=number_of_nodes

=== description
Given the `root` of a **binary search tree**, return the minimum absolute difference between the values of any two different nodes.

=== constraints
- `2 <= number of nodes <= 10^4`
- `0 <= Node.val <= 10^5`

=== hints
- An in-order traversal of a BST visits values in sorted order.
- In a sorted sequence, the closest pair is always adjacent.

=== template
/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * struct TreeNode {
 *     int val;
 *     TreeNode *left;
 *     TreeNode *right;
 *     TreeNode() : val(0), left(nullptr), right(nullptr) {}
 *     TreeNode(int x) : val(x), left(nullptr), right(nullptr) {}
 *     TreeNode(int x, TreeNode *left, TreeNode *right) : val(x), left(left), right(right) {}
 * };
 */
class Solution {
public:
    int getMinimumDifference(TreeNode* root) {

    }
};

=== driver
LC_DRIVER {
    h.solve(&Solution::getMinimumDifference);
    h.gen([](lc::Gen &g, int n) {
        n = std::max(n, 2);
        return lc::lines(g.bst(n, 0, std::max(100000LL, 8LL * n)));
    });
}

=== approach
**In-order traversal** of a BST produces the values in ascending order, and in a sorted list the minimum difference is between neighbours. So traverse in order, remembering the previously visited value, and track the smallest `current - previous`. No array of values is needed: memory is just the recursion stack, O(h).

=== solution
class Solution {
    int prev = -1;           // previous value in in-order (-1 = none yet)
    int best = INT_MAX;

    void inorder(TreeNode *node) {
        if (!node) return;
        inorder(node->left);
        if (prev >= 0) best = min(best, node->val - prev);   // neighbours in sorted order
        prev = node->val;
        inorder(node->right);
    }

public:
    int getMinimumDifference(TreeNode* root) {
        prev = -1;
        best = INT_MAX;
        inorder(root);
        return best;
    }
};

=== examples
[4,2,6,1,3]
> 1

[1,0,48,null,null,12,49]
> 1

=== tests
[1,null,3]
> 2

[100,50]
> 50

[236,104,701,null,227,null,911]
> 9

@@@ problem 230 kth-smallest-element-in-a-bst
title: Kth Smallest Element in a BST
difficulty: Medium
topics: Tree, Depth-First Search, Binary Search Tree, Binary Tree
params: root, k
time: O(h + k)
space: O(h)
bench: n=64..1048576 time=n space=1 unit=number_of_nodes_(k_=_n/2)

=== description
Given the `root` of a binary search tree and an integer `k`, return the `k`-th smallest value (1-indexed) among all node values.

Follow-up: if the tree is modified often and you need the k-th smallest frequently, how would you optimize?

=== constraints
- `1 <= k <= n <= 10^4`
- `0 <= Node.val <= 10^4`

=== hints
- In-order traversal visits values in sorted order; stop after `k` nodes.
- An iterative traversal with an explicit stack makes stopping early easy.

=== template
/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * struct TreeNode {
 *     int val;
 *     TreeNode *left;
 *     TreeNode *right;
 *     TreeNode() : val(0), left(nullptr), right(nullptr) {}
 *     TreeNode(int x) : val(x), left(nullptr), right(nullptr) {}
 *     TreeNode(int x, TreeNode *left, TreeNode *right) : val(x), left(left), right(right) {}
 * };
 */
class Solution {
public:
    int kthSmallest(TreeNode* root, int k) {

    }
};

=== driver
LC_DRIVER {
    h.solve(&Solution::kthSmallest);
    h.gen([](lc::Gen &g, int n) {
        n = std::max(n, 1);
        return lc::lines(g.bst(n, 0, std::max(10000LL, 4LL * n)), n > 40 ? n / 2 : g.irange(1, n));
    });
}

=== approach
Iterative **in-order traversal** with a stack: go left as far as possible pushing nodes, pop the smallest remaining node, count it, and continue with its right subtree. The `k`-th popped node is the answer. We stop immediately, so the work is O(h + k).

For the follow-up, store subtree sizes in each node (an order-statistics tree) to answer in O(h).

=== solution
class Solution {
public:
    int kthSmallest(TreeNode* root, int k) {
        vector<TreeNode*> st;
        TreeNode *cur = root;
        while (true) {
            while (cur) { st.push_back(cur); cur = cur->left; }   // go to the smallest
            cur = st.back(); st.pop_back();
            if (--k == 0) return cur->val;                        // k-th in sorted order
            cur = cur->right;
        }
    }
};

=== examples
[3,1,4,null,2]
1
> 1

[5,3,6,2,4,null,null,1]
3
> 3

=== tests
[1]
1
> 1

[2,1,3]
3
> 3

[5,3,6,2,4,null,null,1]
6
> 6

@@@ problem 98 validate-binary-search-tree
title: Validate Binary Search Tree
difficulty: Medium
topics: Tree, Depth-First Search, Binary Search Tree, Binary Tree
params: root
time: O(n)
space: O(h)
bench: n=64..1048576 time=n space=logn unit=number_of_nodes

=== description
Given the `root` of a binary tree, determine whether it is a valid **binary search tree**:

- the left subtree of a node contains only values **strictly less** than the node's value;
- the right subtree contains only values **strictly greater**;
- both subtrees are also binary search trees.

=== constraints
- `1 <= number of nodes <= 10^4`
- `-2^31 <= Node.val <= 2^31 - 1`

=== hints
- Checking only a node against its direct children is not enough: `[5,4,6,null,null,3,7]` is invalid.
- Pass down the open interval `(low, high)` that the subtree's values must lie in. Use `long long` bounds so `INT_MIN` / `INT_MAX` values work.

=== template
/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * struct TreeNode {
 *     int val;
 *     TreeNode *left;
 *     TreeNode *right;
 *     TreeNode() : val(0), left(nullptr), right(nullptr) {}
 *     TreeNode(int x) : val(x), left(nullptr), right(nullptr) {}
 *     TreeNode(int x, TreeNode *left, TreeNode *right) : val(x), left(left), right(right) {}
 * };
 */
class Solution {
public:
    bool isValidBST(TreeNode* root) {

    }
};

=== driver
LC_DRIVER {
    h.solve(&Solution::isValidBST);
    h.gen([](lc::Gen &g, int n) {
        n = std::max(n, 1);
        bool extreme = g.coin(0.2);
        TreeNode *t = extreme ? g.bst(n, INT_MIN, INT_MAX) : g.bst(n, -3 * n - 5, 3 * n + 5);
        if (n <= 40 && !extreme && g.coin(0.5)) {      // break it somewhere
            TreeNode *p = t;
            while ((p->left || p->right) && g.coin(0.7)) p = (p->left && (g.coin() || !p->right)) ? p->left : p->right;
            p->val += g.coin() ? g.irange(1, 6) : -g.irange(1, 6);
        }
        return lc::lines(t);
    });
}

=== approach
Every node must lie strictly inside an interval determined by its ancestors: going left tightens the upper bound to the parent's value, going right tightens the lower bound. Recurse with `(low, high)` starting from `(-∞, +∞)`. Using `long long` bounds (or null pointers) avoids trouble when node values equal `INT_MIN` or `INT_MAX`.

=== solution
class Solution {
    bool valid(TreeNode *node, long long low, long long high) {
        if (!node) return true;
        if (node->val <= low || node->val >= high) return false;   // outside allowed interval
        return valid(node->left, low, node->val)                   // left: values < node
            && valid(node->right, node->val, high);                // right: values > node
    }

public:
    bool isValidBST(TreeNode* root) {
        return valid(root, LLONG_MIN, LLONG_MAX);
    }
};

=== examples
[2,1,3]
> true

[5,1,4,null,null,3,6]
> false
: The root is 5 but its right child is 4.

=== tests
[1]
> true

[1,1]
> false

[5,4,6,null,null,3,7]
> false

[2147483647]
> true

[-2147483648,null,2147483647]
> true

[3,1,5,0,2,4,6]
> true
